# Plutus Bitcoin Escrow Script — P2WSH 2-of-3 Multisig with CLTV Timeout
#
# This script implements a non-custodial escrow for Bitcoin using
# Pay-to-Witness-Script-Hash (P2WSH) with a 2-of-3 multisig and
# a CheckLockTimeVerify (CLTV) timeout clause.
#
# Participants:
#   - Buyer: funds the escrow
#   - Seller: receives payment on release
#   - Arbitrator: resolves disputes
#
# Spending paths:
#
#   Path 1 — Normal (2-of-3 multisig):
#     Any two of {buyer, seller, arbitrator} can sign to spend.
#     Used for: release (buyer+seller), dispute resolution (buyer+arb or seller+arb).
#
#   Path 2 — Timeout refund (buyer only):
#     After TIMEOUT_BLOCKS, the buyer can unilaterally reclaim funds.
#     Used for: buyer protection when seller disappears.
#
# Script (Bitcoin Script assembly):
#
#   OP_IF
#       # Path 1: 2-of-3 multisig
#       OP_2
#       <buyer_pubkey>
#       <seller_pubkey>
#       <arbitrator_pubkey>
#       OP_3
#       OP_CHECKMULTISIG
#   OP_ELSE
#       # Path 2: timeout refund
#       <timeout_blocks>
#       OP_CHECKLOCKTIMEVERIFY
#       OP_DROP
#       <buyer_pubkey>
#       OP_CHECKSIG
#   OP_ENDIF
#
# Witness stack for Path 1 (multisig):
#   <empty>  <sig1>  <sig2>  <TRUE>  <script>
#
# Witness stack for Path 2 (timeout):
#   <buyer_sig>  <FALSE>  <script>
#
# P2WSH address construction:
#   witness_program = SHA256(script)
#   scriptPubKey = OP_0 <32-byte witness_program>
#   address = bech32_encode("bc", 0, witness_program)  # mainnet
#   address = bech32_encode("tb", 0, witness_program)  # testnet
#
# Security properties:
#   - Non-custodial: funds held by script, not any party
#   - No single party can steal: requires 2 of 3 signatures
#   - Timeout protection: buyer can recover after CLTV expiry
#   - SegWit: P2WSH for lower fees and malleability resistance
#   - Compressed keys: all pubkeys must be 33-byte compressed format
#
# Fee enforcement:
#   Unlike EVM escrows, Bitcoin scripts cannot enforce protocol fees directly.
#   Fee enforcement for BTC escrows is handled at the wallet layer:
#     1. The wallet constructs the spending transaction
#     2. The wallet adds a maintainer fee output
#     3. The wallet's fee-enforcing signer verifies the fee output before signing
#     4. Witnesses attest the settlement transaction includes the correct fee
#
# Integration with Go package:
#   See adapter/btc/escrow_script.go for the Go implementation that compiles
#   this script structure with concrete pubkeys and timeout values.
